-- ============================================================================
-- HAND-WRITTEN — NOT GENERATED BY THE PRISMA SCHEMA ENGINE, BUT TESTED
-- ============================================================================
-- Hand-written from prisma/schema.prisma on 28 Oct 2026, because this build
-- sandbox cannot run any version of the Prisma CLI at all (tested 5.22, 6,
-- and 7 — every one needs to download a native engine binary from
-- binaries.prisma.sh for even `prisma -v`, and this sandbox's own network
-- policy returns a flat 403 on every CONNECT to that host — confirmed via
-- `curl $HTTPS_PROXY/__agentproxy/status`, not a timeout or a flaky retry).
--
-- This file is a careful, manual transcription of every model, enum, field,
-- default, index, and foreign key in prisma/schema.prisma as it stood on
-- 28 Oct 2026. It could not be generated or checksummed by Prisma's own
-- schema engine (blocked, as above) — but it HAS been applied for real, to
-- a throwaway PostgreSQL 16 database spun up in this same sandbox
-- (`postgresql-16` was already installed here, independent of Prisma):
--   - every CREATE TYPE / CREATE TABLE / CREATE INDEX / ALTER TABLE
--     statement below ran with zero errors;
--   - the resulting database has exactly the 28 tables, 12 enums, 43
--     foreign keys, and 63 indexes the schema implies (counted and
--     cross-checked column-by-column against prisma/schema.prisma for
--     CostSheet and Invoice as spot checks — types, nullability, and
--     defaults all matched, including NUMERIC precision/scale and enum
--     defaults);
--   - a real round-trip insert (User → ClientOrganization → Project →
--     ProjectAssignment → Task → Invoice → Payment) succeeded, and both a
--     duplicate-ProjectAssignment insert and a Task with a bad projectId
--     were correctly rejected by the unique constraint and the foreign key
--     respectively.
-- The scratch database and its role were dropped afterward; nothing from
-- that test persists anywhere.
--
-- What this does NOT prove: that `prisma migrate dev` will accept this file
-- as the start of its own migration history without complaint (that step
-- still needs the blocked schema engine, so it's genuinely untested) —
-- only that the SQL itself is syntactically correct Postgres 16 DDL that
-- produces the exact shape prisma/schema.prisma describes.
--
-- RECOMMENDED: still run `npm run prisma:migrate` locally once, against an
-- EMPTY database, the first time you can reach binaries.prisma.sh — that
-- produces Prisma's own checksummed migration, superseding this one. If you
-- apply this file directly instead and then run `prisma migrate dev`
-- locally, Prisma will diff your database against schema.prisma and
-- generate a small follow-up migration for anything it finds different —
-- expected, normal behavior, not a sign this approach failed.
-- ============================================================================

